nL159: \nL160: 【78†Image: @tcoratger†avatars.githubusercontent.com】 【70†tcoratger】 commented 【79†Sep 22, 2026】 •\nL161: \nL162: edited\nL163: \nL164: Loading\nL165: ### Uh oh!\nL166: \nL167: There was an error while loading. Please reload this page.\nL168: \nL169: Copy link\nL170: \nL171: Copy Markdown\nL172: ## What this is\nL173: \nL174: Review of the binary track keeps finding the same shape: a reduction is sound only if the caller does something, the doc says so, and nothing enforces it. This converts the instances in the clear ground into things the compiler or the code rejects, and collects the ones that genuinely cannot be enforced in-crate into one page instead of one per module.\nL175: It reuses the patterns already converted rather than inventing new ones: `#[must_use]` on the type for a droppable claim, a typed rejection for a degenerate shape, and derivation over caller-supplied numbers.\nL176: ## Compiler-enforced use obligations, and what they actually reach\nL177: \nL178: `Point` is what every reduction hands back — the place its challenges landed, and the place the surviving claim has to be discharged. Dropping it is not a weaker check, it is unconditional acceptance.\nL179: `Point` is therefore `#[must_use]`, as are the three residual claim types a caller must discharge against a commitment (`ZerocheckClaim`, `SkipOpeningClaim`, `ZkVerifierHandoff`), following `JaggedDenseClaim`. The attribute does not recurse into struct fields, so each needed its own.\nL180: Review corrected my first description of what that buys, and the correction is in the PR because it matters: a page warning against documented-but-unenforced obligations cannot itself overstate its enforcement. A probe on rustc 1.98 with the lint denied shows it fires on an unused expression only:\nL181: \nL182:     `refused:   verify()?;   verify().unwrap();   verify().expect(\"..\");   verify()?.0;\nL183:     compiles:  let _ = verify()?;   let (_, v) = verify()?;   let v = verify()?.1;\nL184:                verify().ok();   assert!(verify().is_ok());   verify().map(|_| ())?;\nL185:                if let Ok(_) = verify()   drop(verify())   let Claim { value, .. } = ..?;\nL186:     `\nL187: Destructuring is the one to watch, since taking the value and ignoring the point is the natural way to write \"I only need the value\". Nothing reaches inside a `Vec`, and a crate outside this workspace sets its own lint level and gets a warning.\nL188: \nL189: A second probe pins what the attribute adds, given that every field type here is already must-use:\nL190: return shape  | before  | after\nL191: --- | --- | ---\nL192: `(Point<Fp>, Fp)`, concrete field  | already an error, via the field element  | error\nL193: `Point<Fp>`, concrete field  | compiled  | error\nL194: `(Point<T>, T)`, generic field  | compiled  | error\nL195: \nL196: So: a bare point return carries no field element to catch it, and in generic code the lint cannot see through a type parameter. Both were silent before. The page says all of this now.\nL197: The 24 call sites that legitimately want only the sponge advanced say so with `let _ =` and a one-line reason.\nL198: ## Degenerate versus trivial\nL199: \nL200: The first revision of this PR rejected a zero-round generic-degree sumcheck as degenerate. That was wrong, and the distinction it got wrong is the one worth writing down:\nL201: \nL202:     `    degenerate  ->  no challenge is sampled AND nothing downstream pins the result\nL203:         trivial     ->  no challenge is sampled because there is nothing left to fold\nL204:     `\nL205: Only the first deserves a floor. At zero variables the summand is a constant, so the sum over the empty cube is that constant, and the same two checks bracket the run as bracket a longer one: the claimed sum reaches the seed before anything is drawn, and the value handed back is opened at the returned point. Nothing is left unpinned.\nL206: Two callers reach that shape honestly. `p3-word-backend`'s shift reduction folds no word variable when a statement is narrower than one committed element — the relaxation landed in 【80†#2275】 — and `p3-whir`'s closing fold plays no bracket when it has no rounds to run. Rejecting the shape broke the first, which is how CI caught it.\nL207: So the rejection is gone and the reason is written down instead, which the issue explicitly allows. A test pins that the empty run is accepted, that its value is the constant, that two empty runs over different statements still part on the transcript, and that the shape stays canonical (no rounds admits only the empty proof).\nL208: \nL209: Re-checking the two other floors against the same question — is it unsound, or merely small? — found one redundant and one mis-aimed:\nL210:   * Zero folding factor: redundant. `p3-whir` already refuses it with `FoldingFactorError::ZeroFactor`. The guard is dropped, and a test pins that the refusal keeps arriving so nobody re-adds it.\nL211:   * Zero security target: mis-aimed, then too narrow. It is not wrong because it is small. It is wrong because it derives `final_queries == 0`, and queries are the only part of the run that tests proximity — a schedule opening no position accepts any committed function. Review then pointed out my floor guarded one profile constructor while `WhirConfig::new` accepted the same schedule.\nL212: It now lives in `WhirConfig::new_with_domain`, beside `NonRedundantStartingRate`, which refuses zero queries for the other reason and already states the principle. Every route to a config meets it, including the prime-field PCS; the profile arm only maps the error.\nL213: That caught an existing test: `derived_pow_reaches_security_target_per_phase` credits its whole security target to grinding, which leaves the algebraic protocol buying no query. The crate's own `valid_rates_yield_positive_query_counts` asserts the opposite invariant, so the floor is right and the parameters were incidental — its budget now sits below the target, which does not affect the rounding it probes.\nL214: Review then found that I had described the guard more narrowly than it behaves, in all four places it is described. `protocol_security_level` is a saturating difference, so the trigger is `pow_bits >= security_level`, not a zero target — and `128/128` is exactly the fixture above. Described as \"a zero target\", the guard reads as inapplicable to a fixture naming a real security level, which is why that fixture survived.\nL215: Every site now names the mechanism, and the test runs `0/0`, `0/20`, `20/20` and `128/128`.\nL216: Where a small shape is trivial rather than degenerate, it is documented with the reason:\nL217:   * The jagged reduction below a live area of two. A one-cell dense multilinear has no interior to test. The vanishing-selector case, where the terminal equation pins nothing, is exactly what the `#[must_use]` claim rules out.\nL218:   * A claimless `BinaryPcs` opening protocol. A run that claims nothing proves nothing and asserts nothing. It is also load-bearing: it is the only way `security_tests.rs` reaches query sampling with a tampered final codeword, since the terminal check otherwise rejects first.\nL219: The jagged reduction gained a test at its smallest folding shape that fails if no challenge is sampled.\nL220: ## Lookup tuple widths\nL221: \nL222: Every tuple folded into one fraction column is combined under the same `beta` powers, so `[x]` and `[0, x]` fingerprint identically and a bus balances against an entry that was never provided.\nL223: `assert_uniform_tuple_width` ran on one path only: local lookups, at construction. My first fix put a sweep inside the per-bus packing loop — and review found the hole in it. Exclusive lookups are pushed into the packed list before that loop, so their branches never reached it, and the other constructor does not check them either. A width-1 global beside a width-2 exclusive branch packed clean.\nL224: The sweep now runs over every lookup before the sort splits exclusive ones off. Two tests cover the reviewer's cases: the mixed global/exclusive pair, and one group whose own branches are widths 1 and 2.\nL225: That still only sees one AIR, and a bus spans AIRs — senders and receivers cancel in the cross-AIR sum, so two tuples alias without ever sharing a column. `p3-lookup` now owns `check_bus_widths(&[Lookups<F>]) -> Result<usize, LookupError>`, next to `check_multiplicity_height_bound`: it walks every bus across every AIR, exclusive branches and unpacked lookups included, and returns the widest payload, which is the figure a challenge table is built from.\nL226: Neither backend calls it yet. multi-stark's `max_width` also spans local buses, which have no cross-AIR meaning, so the two figures must be reconciled first — recorded as a known gap. The return value says so in its own doc: it is the widest global payload starting from zero, while a challenge table takes the max over locals too and starts from one, so nothing wires one in for the other while the gap is open.\nL227: ## The list\nL228: \nL229: `docs/caller-obligations.md` collects what is left, one obligation per entry with the reason it is an entry rather than a fix:\nL230:   * Transcript ordering — a crate can absorb a value but cannot ask a challenger what it already absorbed, or in what order. This covers `verify_at`/`open_at`'s commitment-and-points obligation, the jagged column heights, `add_claim_at`'s caller-fixed point, and the `p3-bus` \"these columns are already committed\" case.\nL231:   * Properties of the committed witness — a reduction sees field elements, not which alphabet they came from or what the AIR around them constrains. The prime-field-valued premise of the pinned fold, `Count::bounded`'s weight, the exclusive-flag booleanity, the padded-encode zero tail.\nL232:   * Parameters whose other side is invisible — the digest width against `2 * security_level`, and `challenge_uniform_bits_rejecting`'s predicate.\nL233: It also records every reduction's minimum non-degenerate shape in one table, with the degenerate/trivial verdict for each, and is linked from `CONTRIBUTING.md` and the architecture guide.\nL234: ## Follow-ups\nL235: \nL236: Found during the sweep, not landed here. The first two sit in `sumcheck/src/prescribed_pcs.rs` and `challenger/`, which other work is touching; the rest are larger than this PR's scope.\nL237:   * `PrescribedPointPcs::verify_at` returns `Result<Vec<OpeningEvals<EF>>, _>`. `unused_must_use` does not recurse through `Vec`, so `pcs.verify_at(..)?;` discards the claimed column values and compiles — the argument verified, and the only thing it is about was thrown away. All three in-tree consumers do check, each re-implementing the same loop, which is evidence the obligation should be a type. Fix: a `#[must_use]` newtype, or a consuming `verify_at_expecting(.., expected)`.\nL238:   * `observe_opaque` does not bind its value's width. The step records `Length::Scalar` regardless of how many sponge units `C::observe` consumes, so two runs differing only in that width share a fingerprint. This is how commitments enter the transcript. Fix: a length-delimited byte prefix, matching `add_scalars_bounded`. Transcript-format change — invalidates recorded fixtures, so it wants a `DomainSeparator` version bump and should land with the next item.\nL239:   * `challenge_uniform_bits_rejecting` and `challenge_uniform_bits` record the same step (same for the `challenge_extensions` pair). Two protocols with materially different challenge distributions land on the same `pattern_hash`, which is the one thing the seed exists to part. Fix: a discriminating tag on the interaction, as the crate already does to part `challenge_bits` from `challenge_uniform_bits`.\nL240:   * `sumcheck/src/layout/verifier.rs` lets `sum(alpha)` and `constraint(alpha)` take any field element rather than the one `batching_challenge` drew, and lets `add_claim` run after the challenge is drawn — the new claim then gets an alpha power the transcript never covered. Fix: make `batching_challenge` consume `self` and hand back a batched-claims value, which closes both at once.\nL241:   * `Count::provided` sets `weight = 0` unconditionally, exempting the interaction from the height check. Nothing distinguishes a genuinely-provided entry from a query except which `pub` constructor the caller picked.\nL242:   * `lookup` exclusive flags — `lookup_key_exclusive` should emit the booleanity and sum-to-at-most-one constraints itself; today the trace-generation path asserts them on concrete values and the constraint system does not.\nL243:   * `RingSwitchShape::sumcheck_rounds`, `BitRingSwitch::prefix_limit`, `SkipDomain::new` each admit a zero-round shape. Each needs the same degenerate-versus-trivial question asked of it rather than a reflex floor.\nL244:   * `Mmcs` has no digest-width accessor, so the `2 * security_level` obligation cannot be checked. Adding `collision_resistance_bits()` ripples to every implementor.\nL245:   * `MultilinearPcs::commit`'s contract claims \"the conformance tests pin the two against each other\". They do not — `assert_pcs_opening_contract` covers only the univariate `Pcs` trait. A `commit/src/testing/` helper would make the claim true and is purely additive.\nL246: ## Testing\nL247: \nL248: `cargo test` on `p3-word-backend`, `p3-multilinear-util`, `p3-sumcheck`, `p3-binary-pcs`, `p3-lookup`, `p3-whir`, `p3-multi-stark`, `p3-batch-stark`. Every `scripts/check.py lint` check — fmt (nightly), clippy, docs, sort, toml, deps — exits zero.\nL249: \nL250: `word-backend` is deliberately untouched: the fix belongs in whether the guard fires at all, not in that crate's test.\nL251: \nL252: Closes 【81†#2271】\nL253: \nL254: 🤖 Generated with 【82†Claude Code†claude.com】\nL255: \nL256: [Input][Input]\nL257: \nL258: Sorry, something went wrong.\nL259: ### Uh oh!\nL260: \nL261: There was an error while loading. Please reload this page.\nL262: \nL263: 【70†tcoratger】 requested a review from 【83†Nashtare】 as a 【84†code owner】 【85†September 22, 2026 11:18】\nL264: \nL265: 【86†github-actions】 Bot added 【87†documentation】 Improvements or additions to documentation 【88†perf】 Performance improvement 【89†binary】 Related to components of the binary SNARK stack. 【90†multilinear】 Stuff related to multilinear PCS, including p3-whir, p3-multi-stark, p3-multilinear-util, etc... labels 【91†Sep 22, 2026】\nL266: 【83†Nashtare】 mentioned this pull request 【92†Sep 22, 2026】\nL267: \nL268: 【93†feat(sumcheck): authenticate jagged claims and ingest live traces #2285】\nL269: \nL270: Merged\nL271: \nL272: 【83†Nashtare】 requested changes 【94†Sep 22, 2026】\nL273: \nL274: 【76†View reviewed changes】\nL275: ###\nL276: \nL277: 【95†Image: @Nashtare†avatars.githubusercontent.com】 【83†Nashtare】 left a comment\nL278: \nL279: Copy link\nL280: \nL281: Copy Markdown\nL282: \nL283: There was a problem hiding this comment.\nL284: ### Choose a reason for hiding this comment\nL285: \nL286: The reason will be displayed to describe this comment to others. 【96†Learn more†docs.github.com】.\nL287: \nL288: [Input][Input] [Select] Hide\nL289: \nL290: I checked the `#[must_use]` mechanics with a probe, and merged the branch into current main and into each open PR that touches the same crates. I also went through the new floors and the obligations page against the code.\nL291: Here is what holds. `rust.unused_must_use = \"deny\"` is in `[workspace.lints]`, and every workspace member has `[lints] workspace = true`. So inside this workspace an unused `(Point<EF>, EF)` is a compile error, including after `?`, `.unwrap()` and `.expect()`. The net diff adds two runtime rejections, `ProfileError::ZeroQueries` and the width assert in `pack_same_bus_with_degree`. Both are fixed by the configuration and the AIRs, so prover and verifier agree.\nL292: I found no honest shape that either one refuses:\nL293:   * `ZeroQueries` fires only when `security_level <= pow_bits`. Every query count comes from that one difference, and WHIR already refuses rate 0, so at that point all counts are zero at once.\nL294:   * No in-tree AIR can reach the assert, because batch-stark's `lay_out_lookup_challenges` already refuses the same condition.\nL295: The first commit's zero-round rejection is fully reverted: `GenericDegreeProof::verify` behaves as on main, and the error variant is gone. The new tests pass on the merged tree, and replacing the width assert with a no-op makes `pack_refuses_two_tuple_widths_on_one_bus` fail.\nL296: This PR merged with main type-checks for the whole workspace with all targets, and clippy with `-D warnings` is clean on the touched crates. 【93†#2285】, 【97†#2291】 and 【98†#2292】 each merge cleanly with this PR onto 【99†`c36bbb7`】. Each combined tree passes `cargo check --workspace --all-targets`, and clippy `-D warnings` on that PR's crates, so none of them discards a `Point` or trips `double_must_use`. 【100†#2283】, 【101†#2294】 and 【102†#2295】 also merge and check clean.\nL297: 【103†#2272】 already conflicts with main in `multi-stark/src/lib.rs`, which is unrelated to this PR. 【93†#2285】 and this PR both edit `sumcheck/src/jagged/mod.rs` without a textual conflict, but their docs disagree (inline, `layout.rs`).\nL298: What I'd like fixed before approving\nL299:   * The lookup docs now say the width rule is enforced. But p3-lookup only checks one AIR's non-exclusive global lookups, and only when they are packed. None of these panic locally: two AIRs sharing a bus with widths 1 and 2, an exclusive group with mixed branch widths, a width-1 global next to a width-2 exclusive branch on one bus, and lookups that are never packed (inline, `challenges.rs` and `types.rs`). In-tree, batch-stark and multi-stark still catch all of these, so there is no hole there.\nL300: But the out-of-tree consumer the description worries about is exactly who reads this doc, and the obligations page has no entry for the case.\nL301:   * \"A discard is a compile error\" holds only for an unused expression inside this workspace. All of these compile: `let (_, v) = proof.verify(..)?`, `let v = ..?.1`, `.ok()`, `assert!(..is_ok())`, `.map(|_| ())?`, and destructuring a claim with `..`. A downstream crate only gets a warning (inline, `caller-obligations.md`).\nL302: Smaller things\nL303:   * The minimum-shape table covers six reductions. It leaves out the p3-bus product GKR (`ProductGkrShape::new` accepts `log_height = 0`), which is the example 【81†#2271】 opens with, and the multi-stark fractional GKR. Ring switching and the skip domain are deferred. Could the description say \"Part of 【81†#2271】\" instead of closing it?\nL304:   * The description lists `SumcheckData::verify_rounds` among the `Result<(Point<EF>, EF), _>` returns. It returns `Result<Point<EF>, _>`, which is the case the new attribute really adds (see the `caller-obligations.md` comment).\nL305: The other inline comments are non-blocking.\nL306: \nL307: [Input][Input]\nL308: \nL309: Sorry, something went wrong.\nL310: ### Uh oh!\nL311: \nL312: There was an error while loading. Please reload this page.\nL313: \nL314: Comment thread 【104†docs/caller-obligations.md】 Outdated Show resolved Hide resolved\nL315: \nL316: ### Uh oh!\nL317: \nL318: There was an error while loading. Please reload this page.\nL319: \nL320: Comment thread 【105†lookup/src/challenges.rs】 Outdated Show resolved Hide resolved\nL321: \nL322: ### Uh oh!\nL323: \nL324: There was an error while loading. Please reload this page.\nL325: \nL326: Comment thread 【106†lookup/src/types.rs】 Outdated Show resolved Hide resolved\nL327: ### Uh oh!\nL328: \nL329: There was an error while loading. Please reload this page.\nL330: \nL331: Comment thread 【107†sumcheck/src/generic_degree/proof.rs】 Outdated Show resolved Hide resolved\nL332: \nL333: ### Uh oh!\nL334: \nL335: There was an error while loading. Please reload this page.\nL336: \nL337: Comment thread 【108†binary-pcs/src/whir/profile.rs】 Outdated Show resolved Hide resolved\nL338: \nL339: ### Uh oh!\nL340: \nL341: There was an error while loading. Please reload this page.\nL342: \nL343: Comment thread 【109†sumcheck/src/jagged/layout.rs】 Outdated Show resolved Hide resolved\nL344: ### Uh oh!\nL345: \nL346: There was an error while loading. Please reload this page.\nL347: \nL348: 【83†Nashtare】 mentioned this pull request 【110†Sep 22, 2026】\nL349: \nL350: 【98†feat(word,word-backend): prove repeated gadgets as instances of one compiled component #2292】\nL351: \nL352: Merged\nL353: \nL354: 【70†tcoratger】 and others added 3 commits 【111†September 22, 2026 15:16】\nL355: \nL356: ` 【112†feat(sumcheck,binary-pcs,lookup,multilinear-util)!: enforce caller so…】 ` [Button: … ]\nL357: \nL358: [Input] [Input] [Input]\nL359: ` 【112†91f5a6c】 `\nL360: \nL361:     …undness obligations instead of documenting them\nL362: \nL363:     A reduction here is often sound only if the caller does something, the doc says\nL364:     so, and nothing enforces it. This converts the instances found in the clear\nL365:     ground into things the compiler or the code rejects, and collects the ones that\nL366:     genuinely cannot be enforced in-crate into one page instead of one per module.\nL367: \nL368:     Compiler-enforced use obligations. `Point` is what every reduction hands back:\nL369:     the place its challenges landed and the place the surviving claim has to be\nL370:     discharged. Dropping it is not a weaker check, it is unconditional acceptance,\nL371:     so the type is now `#[must_use]` and, under the workspace's denied\nL372:     `unused_must_use`, a discard is a compile error rather than a lint. The lint\nL373:     recurses into tuple elements, so it also covers every\nL374:     `Result<(Point<EF>, EF), _>` the crate returns. The three residual claim types\nL375:     that a caller must discharge against a commitment — `ZerocheckClaim`,\nL376:     `SkipOpeningClaim`, `ZkVerifierHandoff` — get the same attribute, following\nL377:     `JaggedDenseClaim`. The call sites that legitimately want only the sponge\nL378:     advanced now say so with `let _ =` and a reason.\nL379: \nL380:     Degenerate shapes. A reduction with no rounds samples no challenge, still\nL381:     returns a point and a value, and separates nothing.\nL382:     `GenericDegreeProof::verify` now rejects a zero-round run with\nL383:     `GenericDegreeError::NoRounds`: at zero rounds the surviving value is the\nL384:     prover's own claimed sum echoed back at an empty point.\nL385:     `BinaryWhirProfile::config` rejects a zero security target, which every\nL386:     schedule meets, and a zero folding factor, which describes a schedule that\nL387:     never terminates. The jagged reduction's floor is a live area of two, and below\nL388:     it the degenerate case is documented as harmless with the reason, since the\nL389:     vanishing-selector case is exactly what the `#[must_use]` claim rules out. Each\nL390:     of the two reductions whose floor was previously unwritten gained a test at its\nL391:     smallest legal shape that fails if no challenge is sampled.\nL392: \nL393:     Lookup tuple widths. Every tuple folded into one fraction column is combined\nL394:     under the same `beta` powers, so `[x]` and `[0, x]` fingerprint identically.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nfeat(word,word-backend): prove repeated gadgets as instances of one compiled component by tcoratger · Pull Request #2292 · Plonky3/Plonky3 · GitHub (https://github.com/Plonky3/Plonky3/pull/2292)\nciteturn3view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: text/html; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn2view2\",\"id\":94}); Total lines: 572\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nchore: release 0.9.0-rc.2 (#2442) · Plonky3/Plonky3@fe5e46d · GitHub (https://github.com/Plonky3/Plonky3/commit/fe5e46d755b8a7d9069778361e21b33e5978b377)\nciteturn3view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: text/html; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn2view2\",\"id\":86}); Total lines: 296\n"
